WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:01.459
"Jimmy Butler is a bully."

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:02.836
Uh-oh, here we go.

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:05.213
You don’t know me. 

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.173
You only know what I allow you to see. 

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.300
But you were quick to try to call me out about it. 

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.010
I’m just, I’m just sayin’. 

00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:21.104
Hey, it’s Taylor Rooks, and this is Take It There.

00:00:21.104 --> 00:00:23.398
And we are about to take a subway train 

00:00:23.398 --> 00:00:26.401
to Penn Station to get on another train to Philly.

00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:26.985
So let’s go. 

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:38.371
We’re gonna stop at downtown Newark, New Jersey. 

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:40.457
and then we’re gonna go to Philadelphia 30th Street. 

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.585
We are on the train going an hour south to

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:46.504
Philadelphia to interview the enigma that is 

00:00:46.588 --> 00:00:47.589
Jimmy Butler.

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:52.719
He obviously has been in the news a lot 

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:53.928
the past couple months.

00:00:53.928 --> 00:00:55.180
And now he’s playing for the 76ers, 

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:57.390
and we are going to his home 

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.809
to really get to the core of Jimmy Buckets. 

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:05.356
Lots of stuff in here, Jimmy. 

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:07.358
You got a billion things goin’ on. How are you?

00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:09.235
I’m fantastic, how you doin’?

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.528
I’m great, I’m great, I’m great.

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:14.115
So, um, we’ve completely taken over your home.

00:01:14.491 --> 00:01:15.241
For sure.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.035
We’re all here just to figure out,

00:01:17.035 --> 00:01:18.286
“Who is Jimmy Butler?”

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:22.332
It’s what I tell a lot of people: You don’t know me. 

00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:24.793
You only know what I allow you to see 

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:26.336
when it comes to what you read in the media.

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:28.838
And nobody knows what I really do on a daily basis. 

00:01:28.838 --> 00:01:30.548
I’m sure you’ll find out here pretty soon. 

00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:32.050
To say the least, I am myself.

00:01:34.052 --> 00:01:35.470
OK, so for every guest,

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.680
we do a “Take It There” segment. 

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.474
So I’m gonna ask you a series of questions,

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:40.934
and you’re gonna take me to that moment

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.560
or that time. OK?

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:42.894
OK.

00:01:43.186 --> 00:01:47.649
Take me to your potential free-agency 
decision this summer.

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:48.900
I can’t take you there.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.777
When that moment gets here, it’ll be fun, 

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:54.364
but it’ll be a moment that’s too far away to
worry about right now. 

00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:57.075
Take me to a day in Tomball, Texas.

00:01:57.075 --> 00:01:58.701
When I was there, it was kinda like, 

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:01.663
whenever you got bored, 
take your ass outside and do something.

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:03.289
Country-type stuff.

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:07.710
Now, like, we got, like, two
Walmarts, we got a Chick-fil-A.

00:02:08.044 --> 00:02:09.546
Crazy! I don’t even recognize it.

00:02:09.546 --> 00:02:11.714
I think I’m in downtown Houston 
when I go through Tomball.

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:14.300
Take me to your first game of spades. 

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:19.180
Spades, I’ve, I’ve played for a while. 
But memorable game? 

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.642
Whenever I was in college. A lot of cheating 
was going on back in the day though.

00:02:23.059 --> 00:02:24.936
He doesn’t think anyone’s gonna 
be my partner in spades. 

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:26.187
They’re not. And you’re probably trash.

00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:28.523
Little do they know I’m probably better than 
everyone in this house. Like, please.

00:02:28.523 --> 00:02:30.108
La basura. You know what that means?

00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:31.067
Yeah, trash.

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:31.568
Trash.

00:02:32.068 --> 00:02:33.069
All right, time for spades.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:34.070
Lemme cut the cards. 

00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:38.449
You have written “The Winners” and “The Suckas.” 
We’re the winners, y’all are the suckers?

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:39.617
If that’s what you wanna believe.

00:02:39.617 --> 00:02:40.618
OK.

00:02:40.618 --> 00:02:41.619
Were you a good kid, Jimmy?

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:41.953
That’s not my card.

00:02:41.953 --> 00:02:42.829
Get off my card, man.

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:43.788
Or did you get in trouble?

00:02:43.788 --> 00:02:44.789
I don’t get in trouble.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:47.292
But did you just not get caught 
when you got in trouble?

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:48.668
I don’t get in trouble. 

00:02:50.795 --> 00:02:54.424
[Announcer]: Tries to beat him to the spot, 
right shoulder in. That’s close, Mike, though, huh?

00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:57.260
[Announcer]: That is close now. 
Players have to be separated.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.512
[Rooks]: So we can agree that 
you’re confrontational? 

00:02:59.637 --> 00:03:00.597
You’ve described yourself as that.

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:01.264
Oh hell yeah.

00:03:01.264 --> 00:03:01.556
OK. 

00:03:01.556 --> 00:03:02.182
I like it.

00:03:02.432 --> 00:03:07.937
And you say that you feed off of confrontation.
In what ways has that helped your career?

00:03:08.396 --> 00:03:11.900
I think it’s gotten me to the point that 
I am at in my career.

00:03:14.444 --> 00:03:17.989
[Announcer]: Up and in, banks it home and a foul! 
Tough shot from Jimmy Butler.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:22.202
If I was in the NBA and I played for Philadelphia, 
I’d wanna live in the city.

00:03:22.452 --> 00:03:27.415
But Jimmy living so far out is so
indicative of who Jimmy is. 

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:28.666
I don’t bother nobody.

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:29.083
Yeah.

00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:33.338
I stay to myself, me and my guys. 
Every single day, we do the same stuff. 

00:03:33.796 --> 00:03:37.759
We wake up early; we’ll go train. 
We’ll do some Pilates, get my core right. 

00:03:38.134 --> 00:03:41.429
We’ll shoot, crack jokes on each other all day. 

00:03:41.638 --> 00:03:47.060
When I’m in my home, like, I’m carefree. 
Like, I don’t worry about nothin’. 

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.103
I don’t worry about what people say or think. 

00:03:49.354 --> 00:03:51.189
I read an article where it said that you—

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:53.441
Uh-oh! Uh-oh, here we go.

00:03:53.441 --> 00:03:54.192
What does that mean?

00:03:54.192 --> 00:03:55.860
“I read an article.” What article did you read?

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:56.736
Where do you think we goin’?

00:03:56.736 --> 00:04:00.782
That’s what people do. I don’t even know what you’re
finna' to say, but just think about this:

00:04:00.782 --> 00:04:01.783
the article that you read?

00:04:01.783 --> 00:04:02.158
Mhmm.

00:04:02.158 --> 00:04:05.245
They only let you know what they want you to know.

00:04:05.286 --> 00:04:07.455
Well, I think that you’re gonna be very surprised 
by my answer, 

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:08.998
but you were quick to try to call me out about it.

00:04:08.998 --> 00:04:11.084
I’m just, I’m just sayin’. Just throwin’ it out there.

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:15.421
I read an article once that
said that you like to journal.

00:04:15.505 --> 00:04:16.047
Mhmm.

00:04:16.047 --> 00:04:17.298
Is that true? Is that accurate?

00:04:17.298 --> 00:04:18.258
It is, that’s very accurate.

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:19.676
OK, how often do you journal?

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:23.137
I journal a lot. At least once a day. 

00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:26.099
Just because as much is going on in my life 

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:29.936
at any certain time, I need to write it down 
because I may need to vent.

00:04:30.311 --> 00:04:31.896
I’m human like everybody else.

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:37.735
So what are some of the things that you find yourself
journaling about more than others?

00:04:38.194 --> 00:04:40.363
I mean, obviously basketball cuz
that’s a big part of my life. 

00:04:40.697 --> 00:04:44.742
How am I gonna make sure that everybody
in my group of people, 

00:04:44.742 --> 00:04:47.453
in my crew, can put food on the table 
for them and their family?

00:04:47.662 --> 00:04:52.667
That’s the main thing that’s on my mind because
if, if my guys are OK, I know I’m gonna be OK.

00:04:53.418 --> 00:04:54.419
If they’re happy, I’m happy.

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:59.257
OK, so we know what happiness looks like
for you. What does sadness look like to you?

00:04:59.507 --> 00:05:02.927
I’m sittin’ in my room. Don’t knock
on my damn door. Don’t talk to me.

00:05:03.511 --> 00:05:04.721
That’s the gist of it.

00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:04.887
Yeah.

00:05:04.887 --> 00:05:05.555
I’m by myself.

00:05:06.264 --> 00:05:09.809
Tell me the time that sticks out that
you were the most sad.

00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:14.355
I would probably say when I wanted to quit 
basketball forever, at Marquette.

00:05:15.231 --> 00:05:19.277
And I was like, “Yeah, you know what? 
This basketball thing ain’t for me.

00:05:19.277 --> 00:05:24.198
I wish I would have had a journal then to get it
all out instead of just talking to myself in the dark.

00:05:24.198 --> 00:05:28.661
I was like, “Man, I don’t know what to
do, who to talk to, who can relate.”

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:31.539
Well, talk to me about that moment. 
Why did you wanna quit basketball? 

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:31.998
Why was it not for you?

00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:35.835
I mean, you’re so far away from home, and to tell 
you the truth, I didn’t do no research. 

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:38.463
I didn’t know that it snowed. I didn’t know that it 
got cold. I didn’t know anything.

00:05:38.463 --> 00:05:38.880
Mhmm.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.009
So I went up there like I was still in Texas:
basketball shorts, T-shirts, flip-flops

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:46.512
The one person that did help me, though
—I will tell you this—Joe Fulce.

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:47.597
We went to Marquette together.

00:05:47.597 --> 00:05:52.602
So it was like, whenever we had a problem, 
we’re each other’s, kinda, coping mechanism.

00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:55.480
The fact that you just had somebody 
to listen to definitely helped.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.857
Yeah, like I go to therapy every week—

00:05:57.857 --> 00:05:58.149
Yeah.

00:05:58.149 --> 00:05:59.650
And I think that it’s the most amazing thing
just because you’re able to talk—

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:02.111
Whatchu go to therapy for? Like, retail therapy?

00:06:02.111 --> 00:06:02.987
Like, you goin’ shopping?

00:06:02.987 --> 00:06:04.447
No. Like, I go, I sit—

00:06:04.447 --> 00:06:04.822
Like wine therapy?

00:06:04.822 --> 00:06:05.490
I do what we’re doing right now.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:06.366
You sit and drink wine. 

00:06:06.366 --> 00:06:06.866
Yeah.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:07.867
There’s different types of therapy.

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:11.913
But you’re right. Therapy comes in many 
different forms, and it almost seems like that 

00:06:11.913 --> 00:06:15.541
journal’s a therapy. Being able to talk to someone,
even if they aren’t listening to you, is a therapy.

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:16.292
Mhmm.

00:06:16.292 --> 00:06:19.295
But I’m curious if you’ve ever thought 
about doing actual therapy.

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:21.381
Done it before. Yeah, I’ve done it before.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:21.923
Tell me about it.

00:06:23.007 --> 00:06:28.179
I mean, I think there’s a better way for me 
to talk to people and handle things.

00:06:28.471 --> 00:06:30.390
I like when people tell me to, like, 
just stop being a bitch. 

00:06:30.765 --> 00:06:31.182
Like,

00:06:31.182 --> 00:06:31.307
Yeah.

00:06:31.307 --> 00:06:31.891
Suck it up

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:37.814
Ooh! Come here. No!

00:06:37.814 --> 00:06:38.272
[Laughing]

00:06:39.232 --> 00:06:42.068
It gets real feisty. You know, stuff gets thrown.

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:43.069
Wait till I get a good hand.

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:43.736
Names get—

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:44.654
No, I got it.

00:06:44.654 --> 00:06:46.197
Names get just demolished.

00:06:46.447 --> 00:06:47.365
No they didn’t.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:49.575
Yo, you cannot do that!

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:50.284
But you can?!

00:06:50.284 --> 00:06:51.244
I know that though!

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:51.661
No, no, no, but you can?!

00:06:51.661 --> 00:06:52.078
That’s cheating.

00:06:52.203 --> 00:06:53.663
That’s cheating. That’s cheating!

00:06:53.663 --> 00:06:54.664
No it’s not!

00:06:54.956 --> 00:06:58.251
I can say whatever I want. You can’t tell
your partner what led.

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:01.045
How well do you guys know each other as partners?

00:07:01.838 --> 00:07:03.965
Do you know what he’s gonna do before he does it?

00:07:03.965 --> 00:07:06.008
I know I played a spade and
you need to play a spade.

00:07:06.217 --> 00:07:07.093
How well do you know him?

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:08.594
Very.

00:07:09.178 --> 00:07:10.388
And that just comes with time?

00:07:11.347 --> 00:07:14.767
Yeah, just like shooting a free throw.

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:24.444
Whoo, let’s go! Ooh, I got nervous!

00:07:24.694 --> 00:07:26.696
Man, if he played his ace, we had it

00:07:31.075 --> 00:07:34.245
So I have a very serious, hard-hitting question.

00:07:34.328 --> 00:07:36.706
What is up with the Ninja Turtle headband?

00:07:37.165 --> 00:07:38.666
It’s not a Ninja Turtle headband!

00:07:38.666 --> 00:07:39.959
It is a Ninja Turtle headband!

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:40.460
No, it’s not.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:43.379
It’s like the Donatello cuz it is in the back and it flows.

00:07:43.379 --> 00:07:46.007
For one, you coulda, you coulda said Raphael. 

00:07:46.007 --> 00:07:46.674
OK.

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:48.426
Like, all of them wore ‘em. 
Let’s just get that out the way. 

00:07:48.426 --> 00:07:49.927
But that’s not what it is. 

00:07:49.927 --> 00:07:52.180
OK, then tell me. What, why are you doing that?

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:58.019
Kyrie wore it. Jrue wore it. So I was like, 
“You know what? Lemme get in on it.”

00:07:58.811 --> 00:08:01.981
I’d tie that damn thing around my neck
if I wanted to and wear it as a bow.

00:08:04.066 --> 00:08:08.696
What’s it like being on a basketball team 
with a personality like Joel Embiid?

00:08:08.696 --> 00:08:10.448
Different, I can tell you that.

00:08:11.115 --> 00:08:14.785
I like it cuz he got some of “I don’t give a fuck” in him.

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:16.913
Like, “Yo, I’m goin’ at you.”

00:08:16.913 --> 00:08:19.165
But he’ll tell you. Like, that’s what I like about it. 

00:08:19.165 --> 00:08:22.543
Like, “Yeah, all right, we cool, but I’m fittin’
to bust your ass tonight.

00:08:22.543 --> 00:08:26.631
Like, I like that. I like being on 
a team with real competitors. 

00:08:29.634 --> 00:08:31.594
Who controls the music in the locker room?

00:08:31.594 --> 00:08:37.266
Not Amir Johnson. I heard his playlist before 
the game—uh, one of these home games. 

00:08:37.266 --> 00:08:39.560
Trash. Terrible.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:40.895
What’s the mood of your playlist?

00:08:40.895 --> 00:08:46.275
Right now, probably some
Scotty McCreery, Mitchell Tenpenny

00:08:46.275 --> 00:08:52.365
“Drunk Me” is a great song, 
but I think the song on the album is “Walk Like Him.” 

00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:54.700
Phenomenal. Phenomenal.

00:08:54.951 --> 00:08:55.952
These are all country artists.

00:08:55.952 --> 00:08:58.538
Yeah, yeah, yeah. We not talkin’ 
about nothin’ except for country here. 

00:08:59.038 --> 00:09:01.457
What made you start listening to country music? 

00:09:01.457 --> 00:09:04.293
Cuz that seems different than 
what people would expect. 

00:09:04.293 --> 00:09:07.088
It all started just with gettin’
on people’s nerves in college.

00:09:07.588 --> 00:09:11.259
Everybody was wearin’ the Beats headphones 
around their neck 

00:09:11.259 --> 00:09:13.594
instead of on their ears like normal people do.

00:09:14.178 --> 00:09:18.099
And you could hear the music, 
so Lil Wayne from this corner, 

00:09:18.099 --> 00:09:21.811
Wiz Khalifa, Jeezy, Drake, name it.

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:22.895
It was all in there.

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:25.106
And I was just like, “Yo, turn your music down.” 

00:09:25.106 --> 00:09:27.400
And they was like, “No, mind your business. 

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:28.693
Pay attention and listen to 
what’s in your headphones.”

00:09:28.693 --> 00:09:31.112
I said, “Say no more. Gotchu.”

00:09:31.112 --> 00:09:34.407
So next game rolls around, 
and I walk in the same way that they were, 

00:09:34.407 --> 00:09:38.869
with the headphones right here 
blaring Tim McGraw, “Don’t Take the Girl.” 

00:09:38.869 --> 00:09:41.747
And everybody was like, “Turn that shit off!”

00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:44.625
And I was like, “No, hell no.
Remember what y’all just told me. 

00:09:44.625 --> 00:09:46.252
Mind your business and
listen to what’s in your headphones.”

00:09:46.377 --> 00:09:53.467
So I actually started listening to the lyrics, 
and I actually liked the music, so I stuck with it.

00:09:54.385 --> 00:09:57.763
Big bro in town, man. His birthday was this week.

00:09:57.763 --> 00:09:58.639
See that?

00:09:58.639 --> 00:10:03.185
You claim to be a better
football player than Antonio Brown.

00:10:03.185 --> 00:10:04.186
Yes.

00:10:04.186 --> 00:10:07.189
With that being said, I could be in debt $30,000

00:10:07.189 --> 00:10:10.276
to him right now because I tried to
guard him in the red zone and failed.

00:10:10.985 --> 00:10:16.240
One of the best wide receivers in the league
—you bet him $30,000?

00:10:16.657 --> 00:10:18.409
Hypothetically speaking, it started at $10,000.

00:10:18.659 --> 00:10:24.123
He could or could not have went 3-for-3. 
Three times 10 is 30.

00:10:24.415 --> 00:10:26.042
I’m just saying, it could have happened.

00:10:26.208 --> 00:10:29.295
You thought you would best AB once?

00:10:30.546 --> 00:10:31.964
Hypothetically speaking, yes.

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:33.966
Was it a reach?

00:10:34.425 --> 00:10:39.764
Hypothetically, hypothetically, it could have 
hypothetically been a bad way to lose,

00:10:39.764 --> 00:10:41.265
hypothetically, $30,000.

00:10:41.265 --> 00:10:41.849
Yeah.

00:10:41.849 --> 00:10:42.850
Yeah.

00:10:43.517 --> 00:10:44.477
Hey Taylor.

00:10:44.477 --> 00:10:49.565
The bet was he could stop me for three
fade routes from Russell Wilson.

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:52.777
Hey Jim, you know you one of my close friends

00:10:52.777 --> 00:10:54.278
 and your credit always good

00:10:54.278 --> 00:10:58.032
with me, but I think today, you need to pay.

00:10:58.783 --> 00:11:01.869
Pay up buddy it's been three years or just donate

00:11:01.869 --> 00:11:02.828
